{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"reasoning","text":"Good. Now write student-tests.mjs. Need meaningful tests. Let's craft robust tests.\n\nPotential issue in replacement test: The origin function returns a new Promise for each call. We need to store resolve/reject functions externally. Ensure calls increments correctly. Let's write carefully.\n\nTest code:\n```\nimport assert from \"node:assert/strict\";\nimport test from \"node:test\";\n\nimport { createConfigApi } from \"./config-api.mjs\";\nimport { ConfigOriginError, createConfigCache } from \"./config-cache.mjs\";\n\nconst CONFIG = Object.freeze({ version: \"v1\", features: Object.freeze([\"search\"]) });\n\ntest(\"retries after a transient origin failure and the API recovers\", async () => {\n  let calls = 0;\n  const cache = createConfigCache(async () => {\n    calls += 1;\n    if (calls === 1) throw new ConfigOriginError(\"origin timeout\");\n    return CONFIG;\n  });\n  const handle = createConfigApi(cache);\n\n  const failed = await handle({ method: \"GET\", url: \"/api/config/acme\" });\n  const recovered = await handle({ method: \"GET\", url: \"/api/config/acme\" });\n\n  assert.equal(failed.status, 503);\n  assert.equal(failed.headers[\"retry-after\"], \"1\");\n  assert.equal(recovered.status, 200);\n  assert.deepEqual(recovered.body, { config: CONFIG });\n  assert.equal(calls, 2);\n});\n\ntest(\"a stale failure of a replaced load does not delete the replacement\", async () => {\n  let calls = 0;\n  let releaseFirst;\n  let releaseSecond;\n\n  const cache = createConfigCache(async () => {\n    calls += 1;\n    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n      if (calls === 1) {\n        releaseFirst = () => reject(new ConfigOriginError(\"first timeout\"));\n      } else {\n        releaseSecond = () => resolve(CONFIG);\n      }\n    });\n  });\n\n  const first = cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\n  cache.clear(\"acme\");\n  const second = cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\n\n  releaseFirst();\n  await assert.rejects(first, ConfigOriginError);\n\n  releaseSecond();\n  const secondConfig = await second;\n  assert.deepEqual(secondConfig, CONFIG);\n\n  const third = await cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\n  assert.deepEqual(third, CONFIG);\n  assert.equal(calls, 2);\n});\n\ntest(\"invalid origin responses are not cached and are retried\", async () => {\n  let calls = 0;\n  const cache = createConfigCache(async () => {\n    calls += 1;\n    if (calls === 1) {\n      return { version: 123, features: [] };\n    }\n    return CONFIG;\n  });\n\n  await assert.rejects(cache.getConfig(\"acme\"), TypeError);\n  const config = await cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\n\n  assert.deepEqual(config, CONFIG);\n  assert.equal(calls, 2);\n});\n\ntest(\"a failure for one tenant does not evict another tenant's cached config\", async () => {\n  let calls = 0;\n  const cache = createConfigCache(async (tenantId) => {\n    calls += 1;\n    if (tenantId === \"beta\") throw new ConfigOriginError(\"beta down\");\n    return CONFIG;\n  });\n\n  await cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\n  await assert.rejects(cache.getConfig(\"beta\"), ConfigOriginError);\n\n  const acmeAgain = await cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\n\n  assert.deepEqual(acmeAgain, CONFIG);\n  assert.equal(calls, 2);\n});\n```\nGood. Need ensure `releaseSecond` defined before use: second call sets releaseSecond. Good.\n\nBut potential race: after cache.clear and second getConfig, origin call happens synchronously? Actually getConfig is async; at start it checks entries.get, no pending, creates pending, calls loadFromOrigin(key) in Promise.resolve().then. That schedules microtask; releaseSecond variable assigned when loadFromOrigin executes. But our `releaseFirst()` and `releaseSecond()` calls are after both getConfig calls, so by then loadFromOrigin has executed (microtasks processed?) Actually synchronous code after cache.getConfig runs before the microtask queue executes? In JavaScript, Promise.resolve().then executes in microtask after current synchronous code. Since our test code is synchronous until releaseFirst(), loadFromOrigin hasn't run yet. releaseFirst and releaseSecond are not assigned until loadFromOrigin runs. But we call releaseFirst() synchronously after cache.getConfig(\"acme\") and cache.clear. At that point releaseFirst is still undefined! That's a problem.\n\nWait, in first getConfig, pending created, loadFromOrigin scheduled. Since we are still synchronous, it hasn't run. releaseFirst undefined. We need to flush microtasks or design differently. How to make release functions available before test actions? We can define origin load with a deferred object that exposes resolve/reject at call time synchronously? But loadFromOrigin is invoked in a microtask. We can use a helper that returns a promise with externally controlled resolve/reject set in a variable when loadFromOrigin is called, but we cannot call release before that. Alternatively, we can use `await new Promise(setImmediate)` or `await new Promise(resolve=>setImmediate(resolve))` to let microtasks run between getConfig and release. That's acceptable. The test should be robust: after `cache.getConfig(\"acme\")`, await something to allow loadFromOrigin to execute and assign releaseFirst. Similarly after second.\n\nSimplify: Instead of external release functions set inside loadFromOrigin, we can predefine an array of deferreds:\n```\nconst deferreds = [Promise.withResolvers(), Promise.withResolvers()];\n```\nBut loadFromOrigin returns deferreds[calls-1].promise. Then we can reject/resolve via deferreds[i]. However releaseFirst must correspond to first in-flight. Since calls increments. We can do:\n```\nlet calls=0;\nconst deferreds = [Promise.withResolvers(), Promise.withResolvers()];\nconst cache = createConfigCache(async () => {\n   const d = deferreds[calls];\n   calls += 1;\n   return d.promise;\n});\nconst first = cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\nawait new Promise(setImmediate); // allow origin call to start\ncache.clear(\"acme\");\nconst second = cache.getConfig(\"acme\");\nawait new Promise(setImmediate);\ndeferreds[0].reject(new ConfigOriginError(\"first timeout\"));\nawait assert.rejects(first, ConfigOriginError);\ndeferreds[1].resolve(CONFIG);\nconst secondConfig = await second;\n...\n```\nBut `setImmediate` not available in all Node versions? Node has setImmediate. Could use `await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 0));`. Or `await new Promise(setImmediate)`. Simpler: `await new Promise((r) => queueMicrotask(r));` But queueMicrotask runs after current microtasks? Actually queueMicrotask appends; if origin call is pending in microtask queue, awaiting queueMicrotask ensures it runs before our callback. We can use `await new Promise(setImmediate);`. Node test environment has setImmediate. Good.\n\nBut to avoid async complexity, we can design replacement test using synchronous-ish resolved promises? We need first load still running when clear/replace. Could make loadFromOrigin return a promise that never resolves until we call resolve, but we need to expose resolve after call. The deferred array works with setImmediate to flush.\n\nAlternative: Use an async function that awaits a gate promise we control externally. The gate can be defined before. For each call, we can return a promise tied to a gate variable that we set before calling getConfig. But each call needs separate gate. We can predefine two gates:\n```\nlet gate1 = Promise.withResolvers(); let gate2 = Promise.withResolvers();\nlet expected = [gate1, gate2];\nlet idx=0;\nconst cache = createConfigCache(async () => expected[idx++].promise);\n```\nThen call getConfig triggers load; it awaits gate1.promise. We can resolve/reject gate1 later. But loadFromOrigin executes in microtask; by the time we call gate1.reject after flushing, it's fine.\n\nLet's implement with Promise.withResolvers (available Node 22? Node --test current? Should be Node 20 maybe Promise.withResolvers not available? It was added in Node 22. The environment? The test runner from earlier output maybe Node version? Let's check node version."}}
