{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"reasoning","text":"All tests pass. Now update plan and write INCIDENT.md. Need include all required sections. Let's craft.\n\nNeed honest remaining risk or operating limit. Could say: `loadFromOrigin` failures that are not true errors (e.g., network hiccups) still cause all waiting callers to see 503; no automatic retries or backoff beyond `retry-after: 1`. Under high concurrency, a burst of failures could create repeated origin calls if many requests arrive between failure and cleanup? Actually on failure, entry removed after catch. If many requests hit while pending in flight and it fails, all see same error; no repeated origin calls. Good. Remaining risk: if `loadFromOrigin` rejects synchronously (not via async) it's still caught by Promise.resolve().then? yes. If loadFromOrigin throws, chain rejects; catch removes entry. Good.\n\nRemaining risk: The fix relies on identity comparison in catch. If a future change stores a different wrapper promise in the map, the identity check would break and a stale failure could delete a replacement. Or: invalid/slow origin responses are not retried automatically by the cache; clients must retry based on 503 retry-after. Also, the cache has no TTL/expiration, so a once-cached config remains forever unless cleared; origin changes won't be reflected. But that may be expected operating limit. We can mention cache has no expiration; operators must call clear() or restart to refresh configs. That is honest. Also no observability metrics; incident logs are external.\n\nLet's write INCIDENT.md. Keep concise but cover all.\n\nNeed not include code? Include file references. The instructions: must describe user impact, root cause and code/log evidence, why logs misleading, fix, regression coverage, remaining risk. Use plain language.\n\nLet's write."}}
