{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > benchmark/fixtures/T6-rejected-promise-cache/INCIDENT.md <<'EOF'\n# Config-read incident\n\n## Summary\n\nOn 2026-08-23, requests for tenant \"'`acme` began returning 503\n`config_origin_unavailable` after a single origin timeout. The origin recovered\nquickly, but the application kept serving the old failure for several minutes.\n\n## User impact\n\n- All config reads for the affected tenant failed with 503 until the stale\n  failure was cleared.\n- Healthy origin probes did not restore service, so on-call engineers chased\n  the wrong lead (cache vs. network).\n- Callers waiting on the original load all saw the same error, which is\n  expected, but subsequent callers could not retry automatically.\n\n## Root cause and evidence\n\nThe cache in `config-cache.mjs` stores the *in-flight promise* for each tenant\nin a `Map`:\n\n```js\npending = Promise.resolve()\n  .then(() => loadFromOrigin(key))\n  .then(normalizeConfig);\nentries.set(key, pending);\n```\n\nWhen that promise rejects, nothing removes it from the map. Every later\n`getConfig(\"acme\")` finds the same rejected promise and rethrows the original\nerror.\n\nThe incident log proves this:\n\n- `request=cfg-781 attempt=1 cache=miss status=503 error=origin_timeout` — the\n  first origin call times out.\n- `component=origin-probe tenant=acme status=200 latency_ms=31` — the origin\n  is healthy moments later.\n- `request=cfg-782 attempt=2 cache=hit status=503 error=origin_timeout` — the\n  very next request still reports `cache=hit` and the same `origin_timeout`\n  error, because the cache is holding the rejected promise as if it were a\n  valid cached value.\n- `origin_load_count=1 upstream_probe=healthy` confirms only one origin load\n  happened and the upstream was healthy, so the failure lived inside the cache.\n\n## Why the logs were misleading\n\nThe `cache=hit` lines after the first failure look like normal cache behavior,\nand the healthy origin probe made it seem as if the cache entry was valid but\nout of sync. That pointed investigations toward cache invalidation or stale\nTTL rules. In reality, the \"hit\" was a hit on a permanently rejected promise,\nnot a stale success. The log labels did not distinguish a cached rejection from\na cached value.\n\n## Fix and regression coverage\n\nThe repair adds a rejection handler to the cached promise that deletes the map\nentry, but only if that same promise is still the current entry. This prevents\na late failure from a replaced load from wiping out the replacement:\n\n```js\npending.catch(() => {\n  if (entries.get(key) === pending) {\n    entries.delete(key);\n  }\n});\n```\n\nRegressions are covered by `student-tests.mjs`:\n\n- The API recovers from a transient origin failure and returns 200 on the next\n  request.\n- A stale failure of an in-flight load that was cleared and replaced does not\n  delete the replacement entry.\n- Invalid origin responses (TypeError from `normalizeConfig`) are not cached\n  and are retried.\n- A failure for one tenant does not evict a successful cached entry for another\n  tenant.\n\nThe original committed tests in `config-cache.test.mjs` continue to pass,\nincluding coalescing, tenant isolation, copy-on-return, and the existing\nfailure-recovery path.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\nThe cache has no expiration or TTL. Once a config loads successfully, it stays\nin memory indefinitely until `clear()` is called or the process restarts.\nOperators must treat config refresh as an explicit invalidation concern; a\nfuture change that expects automatic refresh will fail silently.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat > benchmark/fixtures/T6-rejected-promise-cache/INCIDENT.md <<'EOF'\n# Config-read incident\n\n## Summary\n\nOn 2026-08-23, requests for tenant \"'`acme` began returning 503\n`config_origin_unavailable` after a single origin timeout. The origin recovered\nquickly, but the application kept serving the old failure for several minutes.\n\n## User impact\n\n- All config reads for the affected tenant failed with 503 until the stale\n  failure was cleared.\n- Healthy origin probes did not restore service, so on-call engineers chased\n  the wrong lead (cache vs. network).\n- Callers waiting on the original load all saw the same error, which is\n  expected, but subsequent callers could not retry automatically.\n\n## Root cause and evidence\n\nThe cache in `config-cache.mjs` stores the *in-flight promise* for each tenant\nin a `Map`:\n\n```js\npending = Promise.resolve()\n  .then(() => loadFromOrigin(key))\n  .then(normalizeConfig);\nentries.set(key, pending);\n```\n\nWhen that promise rejects, nothing removes it from the map. Every later\n`getConfig(\"acme\")` finds the same rejected promise and rethrows the original\nerror.\n\nThe incident log proves this:\n\n- `request=cfg-781 attempt=1 cache=miss status=503 error=origin_timeout` — the\n  first origin call times out.\n- `component=origin-probe tenant=acme status=200 latency_ms=31` — the origin\n  is healthy moments later.\n- `request=cfg-782 attempt=2 cache=hit status=503 error=origin_timeout` — the\n  very next request still reports `cache=hit` and the same `origin_timeout`\n  error, because the cache is holding the rejected promise as if it were a\n  valid cached value.\n- `origin_load_count=1 upstream_probe=healthy` confirms only one origin load\n  happened and the upstream was healthy, so the failure lived inside the cache.\n\n## Why the logs were misleading\n\nThe `cache=hit` lines after the first failure look like normal cache behavior,\nand the healthy origin probe made it seem as if the cache entry was valid but\nout of sync. That pointed investigations toward cache invalidation or stale\nTTL rules. In reality, the \"hit\" was a hit on a permanently rejected promise,\nnot a stale success. The log labels did not distinguish a cached rejection from\na cached value.\n\n## Fix and regression coverage\n\nThe repair adds a rejection handler to the cached promise that deletes the map\nentry, but only if that same promise is still the current entry. This prevents\na late failure from a replaced load from wiping out the replacement:\n\n```js\npending.catch(() => {\n  if (entries.get(key) === pending) {\n    entries.delete(key);\n  }\n});\n```\n\nRegressions are covered by `student-tests.mjs`:\n\n- The API recovers from a transient origin failure and returns 200 on the next\n  request.\n- A stale failure of an in-flight load that was cleared and replaced does not\n  delete the replacement entry.\n- Invalid origin responses (TypeError from `normalizeConfig`) are not cached\n  and are retried.\n- A failure for one tenant does not evict a successful cached entry for another\n  tenant.\n\nThe original committed tests in `config-cache.test.mjs` continue to pass,\nincluding coalescing, tenant isolation, copy-on-return, and the existing\nfailure-recovery path.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\nThe cache has no expiration or TTL. Once a config loads successfully, it stays\nin memory indefinitely until `clear()` is called or the process restarts.\nOperators must treat config refresh as an explicit invalidation concern; a\nfuture change that expects automatic refresh will fail silently.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
